      {
        "heading": "Detection vs. Incident: Reading the Process Tree Instead of a Pile of Alerts",
        "content": "The single most important structural concept in the Falcon console is the difference between a **Detection** and an **Incident**, and confusing the two is the fastest way to under-triage a serious attack. A Detection is Falcon's record of one specific suspicious event — a process that requested access to LSASS memory, a scheduled task created with an unusual name, a PowerShell process launched with an encoded command. Each Detection carries its own severity, its own MITRE ATT&CK tactic and technique tags, its own confidence score, and its own process tree showing exactly what launched what.\n\nBut real attacks are rarely a single event. A phishing document that spawns PowerShell, which downloads a second-stage payload, which dumps credentials, which is then used to move laterally to a second host, can easily generate four, five, or more separate Detections across one or more machines. Reviewing each of those as an isolated case wastes time and, worse, risks an analyst closing three of the four as individually low-severity without ever seeing that together they describe one coherent attack. Falcon's answer to this is the **Incident**: an automatic grouping of related Detections that Falcon's cloud correlation engine determines belong to the same attack chain, based on shared process lineage, shared hosts, shared timing, and shared indicators. Instead of four disconnected cards, you open one Incident and see the full chain end to end, with a single combined severity score reflecting the worst thing that happened anywhere in the chain.\n\n**The process tree is where you actually read the story.** Inside a Detection or an Incident, the process tree is a visual diagram of parent-child relationships: which executable launched which, in order, with the exact command line for each step. This is not decoration — it is frequently the fastest way to distinguish a true positive from a false positive, because the shape of the tree itself carries meaning. A Word document process spawning a PowerShell process is a shape that essentially never occurs during normal document editing; a scheduled task run under the SYSTEM account spawning an unfamiliar binary from a Temp directory is a shape a legitimate administrative task rarely takes. Reading the tree top to bottom lets you reconstruct initial access, execution, and any subsequent steps without pivoting between five different log sources, because Falcon has already assembled them into one connected view.\n\n**What this means for your triage order.** When an Incident contains multiple Detections, do not simply work through them as separate tickets in whatever order they appear. Start from the earliest event in the process tree — that is almost always the initial access point — and read forward chronologically, exactly the way you would read any other attack timeline. Ask, at each step, whether the next process in the chain is a plausible legitimate continuation of the one before it, or a shape that only makes sense if something malicious is directing it. An Incident with a Critical-severity Detection buried in the middle of an otherwise Medium-severity chain still deserves the urgency of the highest severity present, because the chain is one attack, not four unrelated coincidences that happen to share a host.\n\n**A practical caution.** Automatic grouping is powerful, but it is still an algorithm's best guess at correlation, not a guarantee. Two genuinely unrelated Detections on the same host in a short window — a stale credential-dumping tool a red team left behind years ago finally triggering, alongside an unrelated phishing click the same afternoon — can occasionally land in the same Incident. Treat the grouping as a strong starting hypothesis that saves you real time, not as an infallible verdict that relieves you of reading the individual Detections underneath it.",
        "codeExample": "flowchart TD\n  A[\"WINWORD.EXE opens Q3_Invoice.docm\"] --> B[\"powershell.exe -EncodedCommand\"]\n  B --> C[\"Detection 1: Suspicious Office child process\"]\n  B --> D[\"rundll32.exe loads comsvcs.dll against lsass.exe\"]\n  D --> E[\"Detection 2: Credential Access - LSASS Memory\"]\n  C --> F{\"Falcon cloud correlation engine\"}\n  E --> F\n  F --> G[\"One Incident, combined severity, full process tree\"]"
      },
      {
        "heading": "IOA vs. IOC: Why Falcon Bets on Behavior Over Artifacts",
        "content": "CrowdStrike's marketing repeats a specific phrase often enough that it is worth taking seriously rather than dismissing as slogan: Falcon is built to detect **Indicators of Attack (IOAs)**, not just **Indicators of Compromise (IOCs)**. The distinction is not cosmetic, and understanding it changes how you read every detection in the console.\n\nAn **IOC** is a static artifact tied to a specific known-bad thing: a file hash, a malicious IP address, a domain name, a specific registry key a particular malware family creates. IOC-based detection asks a simple question — have I seen this exact artifact before, on a list of known-bad things? It is fast, cheap, and precise when it matches, but it is also trivially easy for an attacker to defeat: recompile the malware and the hash changes completely; stand up new infrastructure and the IP is different; the detection that worked yesterday is blind today, at essentially zero cost to the attacker.\n\nAn **IOA**, by contrast, describes a pattern of behavior and intent rather than a specific artifact — the sequence and context of what a process is actually doing, independent of what tool is doing it. A classic Falcon IOA does not ask 'is this file a known Mimikatz binary,' it asks something closer to 'is an unexpected process requesting broad access to lsass.exe memory shortly after being spawned by an unusual parent process.' That behavioral pattern is what credential dumping actually requires to work — regardless of whether the tool doing it is Mimikatz, a custom-written equivalent, or a legitimate administrative utility being abused for the same purpose. An attacker who swaps tools to evade a hash-based signature gains nothing against an IOA, because the underlying behavior their goal requires does not change; they would have to abandon the technique itself, not just the file.\n\n**Where this shows up in the console.** When you open a Detection in Falcon, the MITRE ATT&CK technique tag — something like T1003.001, OS Credential Dumping: LSASS Memory — usually reflects an IOA-driven detection rather than a hash match. The GrantedAccess field showing 0x1FFFFF (PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS) against lsass.exe is a textbook example: Falcon is not asserting it recognizes the specific tool involved, it is asserting that the access pattern requested is the access pattern credential theft requires, and that pattern is suspicious regardless of which binary requested it. This is precisely why Falcon can catch attacker tooling it has never seen before, including custom or newly compiled malware with no prior reputation anywhere — the behavior gives it away even when the file itself is brand new.\n\n**Custom IOA rules.** Beyond CrowdStrike's built-in IOAs, Falcon lets an organization author its own custom IOA rules for behavior specific to their environment — for example, flagging any process execution from a network share that should never legitimately run code, or any child process of a specific line-of-business application that should never spawn a shell. Writing a good custom IOA requires the same discipline as any detection engineering: name the exact behavioral pattern, not a specific file, so the rule survives the inevitable day an attacker changes their tooling but not their objective.\n\n**Why this matters for your judgment, not just your vocabulary.** When you see an IOA-driven detection with only moderate confidence, resist the instinct to dismiss it because 'nothing came back on the hash.' A clean or unknown hash paired with a genuinely suspicious access pattern is not reassurance — it is often exactly what a competent, freshly built attacker tool looks like, precisely because it has no reputation history yet for you to check.",
        "codeExample": "// IOC vs IOA, side by side\n//\n// IOC (Indicator of Compromise)         IOA (Indicator of Attack)\n// -------------------------------       -------------------------------\n// Asks: seen this EXACT artifact?       Asks: does this BEHAVIOR match\n//   (hash, IP, domain, registry key)      what the attack goal requires?\n// Defeated by: recompile, new infra     Defeated by: abandoning the\n//   -> zero cost to the attacker          TECHNIQUE itself -> real cost\n// Example: SHA256 matches known           Example: unrelated process\n//   Mimikatz sample                        requests PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS\n//                                          (0x1FFFFF) against lsass.exe\n//\n// A clean/unknown hash + a genuinely suspicious IOA is NOT reassurance.\n// It is often what a freshly compiled, never-seen-before tool looks like."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Real Time Response: What You Can Do, and Where the Line Sits",
        "content": "**Real Time Response (RTR)** is Falcon's remote shell into a live endpoint, established over the same encrypted channel the sensor already uses to talk to CrowdStrike's cloud — no VPN, no RDP port opened, no separate remote-access tool installed. For a SOC analyst, RTR is what turns 'I think that laptop in another office is compromised' into an actual investigation you can act on within minutes, without anyone physically touching the machine.\n\n**What RTR is built for.** Inside an RTR session, an analyst runs CrowdStrike-specific commands rather than native cmd or bash. The core categories are investigative: ps to list running processes, netstat to view active network connections, reg query to read registry values, ls and cd to browse the filesystem. From there, RTR supports evidence collection — get pulls a specific file off the endpoint for offline analysis, and higher-privilege sessions can pull a full memory dump for deeper forensic work. It also supports immediate, narrow containment actions: kill terminates a specific malicious process by PID, and rm removes a specific file, such as a dropped payload or a persistence artifact. Higher-privilege operators can also put a script or tool onto the endpoint and run it, which is how many organizations deploy a custom remediation script at scale across dozens of compromised hosts simultaneously rather than touching each one by hand.\n\n**Privilege tiers, on purpose.** Not every Falcon user gets full RTR capability. CrowdStrike separates RTR access into tiers — commonly a read-only investigative tier that can run informational commands like ps and netstat, an active-response tier that adds the ability to kill processes and remove files, and a full administrator tier that adds the ability to put and run scripts. This tiering exists because RTR is powerful enough to do real damage if misused or if an attacker somehow gained access to an analyst's own Falcon account, and restricting who can push arbitrary scripts to production endpoints is a basic operational safeguard, not bureaucratic friction.\n\n**Every action is logged, by design.** Every command typed in an RTR session, and every file retrieved or deleted, is recorded in an audit trail tied to the analyst's identity. This matters in two directions: it protects the organization by creating a defensible record of exactly what was done to a host during an investigation, and it protects the analyst, because 'I ran a command that unexpectedly broke something' is a very different conversation with a full audit log than without one. Treat RTR the way you would treat any action on a production system during an active incident — deliberate, documented in your case notes as you go, not just recoverable after the fact from the audit log.\n\n**What RTR is explicitly not for.** RTR is not a substitute for network containment. Killing a process and deleting a file stops what you can see, but it does nothing about persistence mechanisms you have not found yet, and it does nothing to stop an attacker with an active foothold from simply relaunching. For that, Falcon offers **Network Containment**, a separate action that isolates the host from the network while deliberately preserving its connection to CrowdStrike's cloud — meaning you can still run RTR against a contained host even though it can no longer talk to anything else, including the attacker's infrastructure. The correct sequence in a serious incident is usually contain first to stop the bleeding, then use RTR against the now-isolated host to investigate and collect evidence, rather than leaving a live host connected to the network while you poke around inside it.",
        "codeExample": "// RTR command reference — organized by what each tier actually does\n//\n// READ-ONLY / INVESTIGATIVE TIER\n//   ps                    list running processes\n//   netstat               show active network connections\n//   reg query <key>       read a registry value\n//   ls / cd               browse the filesystem\n//\n// ACTIVE RESPONDER TIER (adds)\n//   get <filepath>        retrieve a file for offline analysis\n//   kill <pid>             terminate a specific process\n//   rm <filepath>          remove a specific file\n//\n// FALCON ADMINISTRATOR TIER (adds)\n//   put <file>             stage a script/tool onto the endpoint\n//   run <script>           execute the staged script\n//\n// EVERY command above is written to an audit trail tied to the analyst.\n//\n// RECOMMENDED SEQUENCE during active compromise:\n//   1. Network Containment  -> isolate host, KEEP cloud channel alive\n//   2. RTR (this session)   -> investigate + collect evidence on the\n//                              now-isolated host, safely, with logging"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Prevention Policies: Detect-Only Is Not Protection, It Only Looks Like It",
        "content": "Every Host Group in Falcon is governed by a **Prevention Policy**, and the single most consequential setting inside it is whether the group runs in **Prevent** mode or **Detect-only** mode. The distinction sounds simple, but the consequence of getting it wrong is one of the most common and most dangerous gaps a SOC inherits from whoever configured the environment before them.\n\n**In Prevent mode**, when Falcon's machine learning models or IOA engine score a process as sufficiently malicious, the sensor blocks it automatically — the process is killed, or never allowed to execute in the first place, before it does anything further. **In Detect-only mode**, the exact same scoring happens, the exact same Detection appears in the console with the exact same severity and confidence, but nothing is blocked. The malicious process runs to completion, unopposed, while a card describing what it did waits for an analyst to notice it.\n\n**Why Detect-only exists at all.** This is a legitimate mode, not a bug — organizations deploying Falcon for the first time commonly run new Host Groups in Detect-only for a calibration period, watching what the ML models would have blocked before committing to automatic prevention, specifically to catch false positives that would otherwise disrupt business-critical software before trust in the tuning is established. Some highly custom or fragile legacy environments also stay in Detect-only permanently because a false block on a critical process is judged more disruptive than the residual risk, a genuine risk tradeoff a security team can reasonably choose to make with eyes open.\n\n**Why it becomes dangerous.** The failure mode is not the existence of Detect-only mode — it is a Host Group left in Detect-only by default, by oversight, or because the calibration period that was supposed to be temporary was simply never revisited, while everyone downstream assumes 'we have EDR deployed there' means 'we are protected there.' A SOC that only ever looks at the Detections page sees exactly the same volume and severity of alerts whether a host is in Prevent or Detect-only, because Falcon still generates and displays every Detection either way — the console gives you no loud, unmissable signal that protection was actually absent unless you specifically go check the policy. This is precisely why a ransomware operator who lands on a Detect-only host can execute their entire encryption routine while Falcon faithfully logs every step of it, generating a perfect forensic record of an attack nobody stopped.\n\n**The tuning dial within Prevent.** Even within Prevent mode, Falcon exposes a machine-learning sensitivity slider — commonly labeled across levels like Cautious, Moderate, Aggressive, and Extra Aggressive — that trades false-positive risk against detection aggressiveness. A Cautious setting blocks only the highest-confidence matches, minimizing disruption to legitimate software at the cost of letting more borderline-malicious activity through undetected; an Extra Aggressive setting blocks far more readily, catching more real threats at the cost of more false positives against unusual-but-legitimate business software. There is no universally correct setting — the right level depends on the asset's criticality and the organization's tolerance for a blocked legitimate process versus a missed malicious one, which is exactly why different Host Groups should carry different policies rather than one setting applied blindly everywhere.\n\n**What this means for you as an analyst.** When you close a Detection as handled, or when you are surprised that an attack you expected Falcon to stop actually completed, the very first thing to check — before questioning the detection engine's competence — is which Prevention Policy governs that specific Host Group, and whether it was ever set to Prevent at all. A detection with a perfect severity score and full ATT&CK mapping tells you Falcon understood the attack correctly. It tells you nothing about whether Falcon was allowed to stop it.",
        "codeExample": "flowchart TD\n  A[\"Falcon scores a running process as malicious\"] --> B{\"Prevention Policy mode for this Host Group?\"}\n  B -->|\"Detect-only\"| C[\"Detection logged, process runs to completion\"]\n  B -->|\"Prevent\"| D{\"ML confidence above policy threshold?\"}\n  D -->|\"Yes\"| E[\"Process blocked automatically before impact\"]\n  D -->|\"No / below threshold\"| F[\"Detection logged for analyst review only\"]\n  C --> G[\"Attack completes; analyst finds a perfect forensic record, too late\"]\n  E --> H[\"Attack stopped; analyst reviews and closes the case\"]"
      }
